{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); k=\\\"v9\\\"; print(x[k][\\\"question\\\"]); exec(\\\"for c in x[k][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n i=c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]; row=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",i,c[\\\\\\\"day\\\\\\\"]); t=row[0] if row else \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"; lines=re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in lines if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|2026|h200|approval|authoriz|commit|backstop|financ|billion|contract|pricing|prices|ASIC|custom\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); k=\\\"v8\\\"; print(x[k][\\\"question\\\"]); exec(\\\"for c in x[k][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n i=c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]; row=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",i,c[\\\\\\\"day\\\\\\\"]); t=row[0] if row else \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"; lines=re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in lines if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|2026|h200|approval|authoriz|commit|backstop|financ|billion|contract|pricing|prices|ASIC|custom\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?\n\n### gnews:a6ed98dfdf1ad76f6edffdfd 2026-07-02\n\n\n### gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02\nBefore Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S.\nPresident Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December.\nBy 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\nMcGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, even if Huawei were to make 2 million AI chips this year and 4 million in 2027.\nYet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim.\nThe Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\n\n### gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06\nNvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nNVIDIA's NVLink Fusion pivot keeps its interconnect sticky even as hyperscalers shift compute to custom chips, but the strategy implicitly concedes the battle over GPUs.\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\nQ1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\nManagement guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%.\nNetworking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\nThe customer list is the threat.\nAmazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative.\nCustom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material.\nSupply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\nNVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\nA company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise.\nRoughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom silicon head-on loses.\nBuy conviction requires durable evidence that networking and software capture margin even when compute goes custom, plus a China resolution.\nBuying it aggressively requires believing the customer base will keep writing checks it is openly trying to stop writing.\n\n### gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06\n“China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\nChina is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\n\n### gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07\nAmid US restrictions on AI chip exports to China, Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.\nReuters previously reported that the company was preparing its first funding round worth US$7 billion, with a valuation of between US$52 billion and US$59 billion, ending a years-long strategy of rejecting outside investment.\n\n### gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08\nChina to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\nChina will allow top AI companies like Alibaba and ByteDance to buy limited amounts of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions.\nChinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\nChina plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\nThe companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.\nThough Nvidia is now up 9.4 per cent in 2026 , that pales in comparison with the gains of most major semiconductor peers.\nThe H200 import snags hinged in part on Chinese concerns that a flood of American-designed AI processors would hinder the government’s long-held goal of developing an indigenous chip industry.\nBut soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\nNvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\nThe Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported.\nIn May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\n\n### gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08\n“China’s drive to substitute locally made AI-semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic markers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\nChina is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) to build data centers across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\n\n### gnews:50a84fa6b2f3eb33f29948a4 2026-07-09\n1 China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia H200 chips\n+ China had previously withheld approval despite US authorization.\nPlus: Meta will pay up to $18 billion to settle a landmark child-safety case.\nFrom event sponsorships to custom content to visually arresting video storytelling, advertising with MIT Technology Review creates opportunities for your brand to resonate with an unmatched audience of technology and business elite.\n\n### gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\nQuanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors.\nWhile this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\n\n### gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13 2026-07-09\nWhy China is finally letting AI firms buy the Nvidia H200\nIn early 2026, Washington took the rare step of approving Nvidia’s H200 graphics processing unit for export to China, but Beijing has deliberately restricted Chinese firms from purchasing them as it pursues a tech self-sufficiency drive.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); k=\\\"v5\\\"; print(x[k][\\\"question\\\"]); exec(\\\"for c in x[k][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n i=c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]; row=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",i,c[\\\\\\\"day\\\\\\\"]); t=row[0] if row else \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"; lines=re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in lines if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|2026|h200|approval|authoriz|commit|backstop|financ|billion|contract|pricing|prices|ASIC|custom\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?\n\n### gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5 2026-07-02\nIt must disclose its full lifecycle emissions and legally commit, alongside its Asian manufacturing partners, to producing 100 percent carbon-free AI chips by 2030.\n\n### gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2 2026-07-05\nAs the DRAM crisis intensifies in 2026, graphics card prices continue to go up.\nSo as of right now, both US and European retailers are restocking the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with prices starting at $329 and €333, respectively.\nWe have seen prices for components and products swell sharply over the past few months, and everyone is signaling an intensification of this trend in the coming years .\nWhen looking at the prices of existing models, the RTX 5060 8 GB is available at $349-$359 .\nAnd since DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements , we believe the crisis won't end soon, despite all DRAM manufacturers making promises to pour billions of dollars towards new manufacturing facilities , which are only being built to meet the demand created by AI, and would likely not push the consumer market out of these dire times.\n\n### gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026.\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026.\nIntel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\nRevenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\nNVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\nBroadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\nAMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\nSamsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history.\nQ3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\nSome estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\nDemand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds.\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\nI write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\n\n### gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06\nMicron’s stock is up about 855 percent in the last 12 months, while Nvidia traded down over 15 percent from its June 2, 2026, high.\nIn fairness, June 2026 was a harsh month for many technology stocks.\nInterestingly, the S&P 500 is up about 9.5 percent year to date, but over the same period, the forward price-to-earnings ratio has contracted nearly 10 percent.\nThat is because earnings of the companies in that index have been incredible, with roughly an 18 percent growth rate for the first half of 2026, and Q2 expected to be above 20 percent.\nThat trend—rising stock prices and simultaneously improving valuations—is very bullish.\nFor earnings to keep improving, we need 1) lower oil prices (which seems to be happening), 2) the AI-related infrastructure buildout to continue (I suspect it will through the next 12 months), and 3) interest rates to stay where they are, or go lower (but the futures market says they will go higher).\nFor its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion.\nOne year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion.\nGAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago.\nGoldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\nUnfortunately, the AI boom is not just pushing up prices for the winners.\nA shortage of memory might affect the prices of consumer electronics.\nI’d say Micron is not the new Nvidia, just a new opportunity (talk to your financial advisor before making any investment).\nAllen Harris is an owner of Berkshire Money Management in Great Barrington and Dalton, managing more than $1 billion of investments.\n\n### gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06\n“China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\nChina is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\n\n### gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06\nThis shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nMemory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\nFor any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\nFirst shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nContract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\nBudgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\nAs rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts.\nNvidia's $7.8 million Vera Rubin rack rewrites AI profit map as GPU share shrinks ↗ · BigGo Finance\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record.\nExplore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\nThe same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for.\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older.\nSchneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\n\n### gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07\nMicron Technology ( MU +4.26% ) has had a phenomenal 2026 so far, rising around 240%, though it was up around 300% until a few days ago before artificial intelligence (AI)-centric stocks started to sell off.\nAs a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\nAs mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half.\nThat could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\nWhile the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\nNext quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\nMicron operates on a non-standard fiscal year calendar, and its FY 2026 ends in August.\nSo, I'll utilize FY 2027 projections.\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 7, 2026 at 9:30AM EST\n\n### gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08\nEvery Nvidia debate now sounds crowded: HBM costs are rising, hyperscalers are building custom chips, the stock is widely owned, and ecosystem financing is raising questions.\nBofA’s latest report flips the setup: what if the market is already pricing in too much fear?\nBofA's valuation table also shows Nvidia at 15.7 times CY2027 earnings and 12.4 times CY2028 earnings, while Apple, Microsoft, Google, Amazon and Meta average 22.3 times and 19.0 times.\nBofA’s argument is that investors are already treating Nvidia as if margins, competition or customer financing will materially damage earnings power.\nBofA does not deny the cost pressure, but argues the market is missing the offsetting pricing power.\nThe report estimates HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 million for Vera Rubin.\nCustom chips are another concern, but BofA’s counterpoint is historical: Google TPU launched in 2015, Amazon Trainium in 2020 and Meta MTIA in 2023, yet Nvidia GPU accelerator sales have grown about 700 times since 2015.\nBofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\nStrategic investments across OpenAI, Anthropic, Intel, CoreWeave, Nebius, Lumentum, Coherent, Marvell and others also raise a fair question: is Nvidia financing its own demand?\nIt estimates total ecosystem investments at around $65 billion, less than 35% of CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of CY2027E free cash flow.\nThe bear case needs evidence that HBM costs, ASICs or vendor financing are hitting EPS, not just narrative pressure.\n\n### gnews:c2ccca1a61b99adc685b1aa2 2026-07-08\nThe AI-cloud firm Parasail will be its first customer, with the system due online later this year.\nSay specialised inference chips take off, like the custom silicon now spreading across big tech.\nEither way, customers stay inside Nvidia’s orbit.\n\n### gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08\n“China’s drive to substitute locally made AI-semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic markers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\nChina is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan ($294 billion) to build data centers across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); k=\\\"v16\\\"; print(x[k][\\\"question\\\"]); exec(\\\"for c in x[k][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n i=c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]; row=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",i,c[\\\\\\\"day\\\\\\\"]); t=row[0] if row else \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"; lines=re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in lines if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|2026|h200|approval|authoriz|commit|backstop|financ|billion|contract|pricing|prices|ASIC|custom\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\n\n### gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01\nCore catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft.\nQ1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix.\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges).\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Development\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis.\nThe companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing.\nThat represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term.\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\nWhile INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\nGoogle deal: Multiyear, locked pricing 3–5 years, custom ASIC IPU co-development\nNext earnings: July 23, 2026\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\nWhat Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance?\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY).\nIntel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion.\n\"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\" said CFO Zinsner.\nThe majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A.\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\nQ1 2026 was Data Center and AI $5.1 billion (+22%), with an average of 60% AI-linked of the mix.\nConfirmation above $150.40 will send prices to a $159.90 price target.\nQ2 2026 guidance to be issued at July 23 earnings is the next checkpoint, where we'll hopefully see a large component of the stack materialize in guidance.\n\n### gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026.\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026.\nIntel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\nRevenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\nNVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\nBroadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\nAMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\nSamsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history.\nQ3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\nSome estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\nDemand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds.\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\nI write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\n\n### gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06\nNvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nNVIDIA's NVLink Fusion pivot keeps its interconnect sticky even as hyperscalers shift compute to custom chips, but the strategy implicitly concedes the battle over GPUs.\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\nQ1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\nManagement guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%.\nNetworking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\nThe customer list is the threat.\nAmazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative.\nCustom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material.\nSupply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\nNVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\nA company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise.\nRoughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom silicon head-on loses.\nBuy conviction requires durable evidence that networking and software capture margin even when compute goes custom, plus a China resolution.\nBuying it aggressively requires believing the customer base will keep writing checks it is openly trying to stop writing.\n\n### gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07\nAmid US restrictions on AI chip exports to China, Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.\nReuters previously reported that the company was preparing its first funding round worth US$7 billion, with a valuation of between US$52 billion and US$59 billion, ending a years-long strategy of rejecting outside investment.\n\n### gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07\nSemiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed,…\nGoogle Cloud revenue surged 63% with backlog nearly doubling to $460 billion, strengthening its in-house TPU case as NVIDIA's Kyber slip extends to 2028.\nSemiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.\nThe delay raises a broader question: whether NVIDIA’s annual cadence, built to stay ahead of AMD and custom silicon, is hitting physical manufacturing limits.\nData Center revenue reached $75.246 billion (+92% YoY) in Q1 FY27, and CEO Jensen Huang described “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history”.\nCEO Lisa Su has said “customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.”\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) benefits through custom ASICs.\nQ2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion.\nBloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\nGoogle ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor.\nGoogle Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\nNVIDIA still commands approximately 81% of the AI chip market , and GuruFocus analysis flags NVDA as 45% undervalued relative to GF Value at current prices.\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\nI write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\n\n### gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09\nThe basic reason is simple: The GPU shortage that looked so alarming last year has eased off a bit.\nWithout the chips or the companies changing too much, the service they provide suddenly became very valuable — and since demand is growing faster than anyone can scale up supply, they have been able to increase prices tenfold over the past year.\nThis, via Datatrack, is what the spot price for DRAM — the price buyers pay for chips on the open market, as opposed to long-term contract rates — looks like since 2023:\nGoogle , Amazon , Microsoft , and even OpenAI have launched their own custom processors to lessen their dependence on Nvidia; even if those chips aren’t as good as the latest model from Nvidia, they’re good enough to drive down the price of compute.\n\n### gnews:7620a6ac8ec29bf19328a734 2026-07-09\nThat dominance is what's pushing companies to spend billions designing custom chips instead.\nGoogle is seven generations deep into its custom AI chip program.\nAny Google Cloud customer in North America and Europe can already rent access to it.\nHe said it had grown into a multibillion-dollar business at a 150% quarterly clip.\nIt announced Maia 200, a chip built for that work, in January 2026, and the chip is already reaching a handful of U.S.\nA custom software layer runs on top of that network, scaling up to clusters of 6,144 chips.\nThe next one, MTIA 450, arrives in early 2027, doubling how fast the chip moves data in and out of memory.\nIt announced a partnership with Broadcom in October 2025 to build 10 gigawatts of custom AI chips, a measure of how much power the new chips will draw once installed.\nInstalling them is set to begin in the second half of 2026 and run through the end of 2029.\nTesla finished the design of its AI5 inference chip in April 2026, according to Reuters .\nTesla is targeting mid-2027 for mass production.\nSpaceX, Musk's other property, filed plans in May for a $55 billion chip manufacturing plant in Texas called Terafab, Reuters reported .\nTotal investment in the plant could reach $119 billion.\nThe company reported $5.2 billion in AI-related revenue for the third quarter of its 2025 fiscal year.\n\n### gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\nQuanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors.\nWhile this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\n\n### gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09\nIt also announced a new $80 billion stock buyback program, in addition to an existing $39 billion program left over from a prior authorization.\nThey include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\n\"The setup is really, really great from a technical perspective,\" StockBrokers.com strategist Jessica Inskip said on Yahoo Finance's Opening Bid (see video above).\n\n### gnews:09b9d76297a57f198651c5e7 2026-07-10\nReuters said Meta plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing.\nThe development reflects a broader industry trend of large technology companies designing custom chips to improve efficiency and manage computing costs.\nHowever, Nvidia continues to hold a leading position in AI training hardware, while many custom processors remain focused on inference workloads, which generate responses from trained AI models.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); k=\\\"v9\\\"; print(x[k][\\\"question\\\"]); exec(\\\"for c in x[k][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n i=c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]; row=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",i,c[\\\\\\\"day\\\\\\\"]); t=row[0] if row else \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"; lines=re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in lines if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|2026|h200|approval|authoriz|commit|backstop|financ|billion|contract|pricing|prices|ASIC|custom\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\n\n### gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01\nIt has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the AI computing ecosystem by offering financial backing to emerging cloud service providers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\nOn July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract.\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\nThe core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\nThese deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement.\nUnderlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing\nA data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.”\nIn other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction.\nStrategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars.\nIn September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\nEach layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.\n\n### gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01\nCore catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft.\nQ1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix.\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges).\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Development\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis.\nThe companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing.\nThat represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term.\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\nWhile INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\nGoogle deal: Multiyear, locked pricing 3–5 years, custom ASIC IPU co-development\nNext earnings: July 23, 2026\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\nWhat Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance?\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY).\nIntel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion.\n\"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\" said CFO Zinsner.\nThe majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A.\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\nQ1 2026 was Data Center and AI $5.1 billion (+22%), with an average of 60% AI-linked of the mix.\nConfirmation above $150.40 will send prices to a $159.90 price target.\nQ2 2026 guidance to be issued at July 23 earnings is the next checkpoint, where we'll hopefully see a large component of the stack materialize in guidance.\n\n### gnews:cdd5ccd6fd57b79e703bfb7d 2026-07-01\nThe company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \"usagelinked earnings stream\" for itself, chief financial officer Colette Kress wrote in a blog post on Wednesday.\n\n### nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\n“Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\n\n### gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02\nNvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\nIn a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves.\nIn practice, cash-poor AI companies trade a slice of whatever they eventually earn for tokens today, while a supplier already running roughly 75% gross margins reaches into its customers’ income statements for a second helping of cash.\nUnder the structure, participating AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure and sell Nvidia-powered cloud services to end customers.\nIt’s no secret that the credit-support side of this model will help to address a financing gap that Nvidia itself has identified.\nEven signed, long-term customer commitments have failed to convince lenders to fund large-scale deployments, leaving smaller clouds unable to borrow against the demand they had already generated.\nNvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\n\n### gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02\n— NVIDIA (@nvidia) July 2, 2026\n— Coin Bureau (@coinbureau) July 2, 2026\n— SixSigmaCapital (@SixSigmaCapital) July 2, 2026\nThis article serves informational purposes only and does not constitute financial advice.\nWeekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\nMeta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\n\n### gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nContracts like these ensure that Nvidia maintains a diversified customer base and also expands its internal compute capacity for researchers when GPUs are left idle.\nThe newer contracts have the added benefit of generating income for Nvidia based on the neoclouds' success, effectively creating a win-win situation.\n\n### gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02\nNvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\nRevenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end customers.\nKress, writing in a blog post, called the resulting income a \"recurring, usage-linked earnings stream\" for Nvidia ; on the customer side, she said the model removes the need to navigate site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up before accessing large-scale compute.\nThe company said AI-native firms including Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI represent the kind of customers the program is designed to serve.\nThe company said emerging AI companies have historically had limited access to capital-intensive compute resources, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing.\nThe move comes as Nvidia contends with growing competition from custom AI chips developed by large cloud providers.\nCompanies including Alphabet $GOOGL and Amazon $AMZN have been offering access to their own processors, putting pressure on Nvidia to build a broader customer base beyond its largest buyers.\n\n### gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03\nNvidia will act as a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity [1]\nNvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \"AI Compute Partnership,\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] .\nThe backstop mechanism works by Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, effectively de-risking lender exposure and making it easier for smaller cloud operators to secure capital for large GPU deployments [1] [3] .\nThe structure echoes — but formalizes — earlier arrangements in which Nvidia provided demand guarantees to help neoclouds like CoreWeave and Lambda raise billions in debt financing [1] .\nIf a neocloud partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing the downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors [1] [3] .\nThe model addresses a persistent bottleneck in the neocloud sector: smaller operators often have customer demand in hand but cannot secure financing quickly enough to build infrastructure, because lenders view GPU residual values as uncertain [4] .\nBy backstopping idle capacity, Nvidia effectively underwrites the asset, making GPU clusters more bankable.\nSharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .\nThe company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory and has partnered with VAST Data for 600PB of storage infrastructure supporting roughly 100,000 GPUs across its sovereign cloud operations in Australia and the Asia-Pacific region [6] .\nPrior to this formalization, Nvidia had provided demand guarantees and customer referrals to help neoclouds raise debt — CoreWeave secured $6.3 billion and Lambda $1.5 billion through arrangements in which Nvidia played a supporting role [1] .\nThe new model makes Nvidia's financial participation explicit, with the company now directly sharing in its customers' cloud economics.\nAnalysts have drawn comparisons to GE Capital's equipment financing model, noting both the upside of recurring income and the counterparty risk if neocloud operators face financial stress [4] .\nThe financing model keeps a broader ecosystem of independent GPU cloud providers alive rather than concentrating AI compute inside the hyperscalers — a strategic imperative for Nvidia, which benefits from having multiple distribution channels for its hardware [3] [4] .\nBy making GPU clusters more financeable, Nvidia may accelerate buildout timelines for operators that would otherwise face 12- to 18-month fundraising cycles.\nThe structure also gives Nvidia optionality: in a demand downturn, the company gains access to GPU capacity it can deploy for its own inference services or sublease, effectively building a buffer inventory at customer expense.\nSupporters counter that it aligns incentives, since Nvidia now has a direct financial stake in its customers' commercial success.\n\n### gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04\nNvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of the financing structure.\nNvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers.\nUnder this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\nThis approach offers AI cloud operators a viable means to finance large installations, which have traditionally been challenging to fund, especially for emerging companies lacking the balance sheet strength typically required for capital-intensive projects.\nThese projects exemplify the scale of infrastructure Nvidia aims to facilitate through this financing model, which is linked to future usage rather than relying solely on conventional long-term commitments.\nNvidia describes these facilities as DSX AI factories tailored to customer demand in various regions.\nNvidia's strategy aims to alleviate this bottleneck by aligning its financial interests more closely with cloud providers serving these customers.\nThese entities depend on reliable access to accelerated computing as customer usage increases, often requiring more adaptable commercial arrangements than traditional data center buildouts or fixed infrastructure commitments allow.\nHe added that the Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory to enable more customers to access the compute necessary for building and scaling AI.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); k=\\\"v13\\\"; print(x[k][\\\"question\\\"]); exec(\\\"for c in x[k][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n i=c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]; row=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",i,c[\\\\\\\"day\\\\\\\"]); t=row[0] if row else \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"; lines=re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in lines if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|2026|h200|approval|authoriz|commit|backstop|financ|billion|contract|pricing|prices|ASIC|custom\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); k=\\\"v13\\\"; print(x[k][\\\"question\\\"]); exec(\\\"for c in x[k][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n i=c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]; row=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",i,c[\\\\\\\"day\\\\\\\"]); t=row[0] if row else \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"; lines=re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t); print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in lines if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|2026|h200|approval|authoriz|commit|backstop|financ|billion|contract|pricing|prices|ASIC|custom\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\n\n### gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02\n— NVIDIA (@nvidia) July 2, 2026\n— Coin Bureau (@coinbureau) July 2, 2026\n— SixSigmaCapital (@SixSigmaCapital) July 2, 2026\nThis article serves informational purposes only and does not constitute financial advice.\nWeekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\nMeta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\n\n### gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04\nHowever, 2026 hasn't been that way.\nThe stock is up 5% so far in 2026, which isn't nothing, but compared to the returns that Nvidia investors have enjoyed in recent years, it feels like an absolute failure.\nOn another note, its market-crushing status is in jeopardy for 2026, as the S&P 500 is up nearly 10% for the year.\nAlthough it doesn't own any of the fabrication process itself, it works with several companies that manufacture its GPUs, which it then sells to a wide range of customers.\nThe big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\nThis conveys that the market believes Nvidia will be a market-average stock after 2026's growth is accounted for.\n\n### gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026.\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026.\nIntel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\nRevenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\nNVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\nBroadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\nAMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\nSamsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history.\nQ3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\nSome estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\nDemand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds.\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\nI write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\n\n### gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06\nWith over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn't just an engineering story — it's a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout.\nBut there's a significant legal and regulatory gap between a DOE demonstration authorization and an actual commercial license, and how Valar navigates that gap will determine whether this moment becomes a milestone or just a very expensive photo op.\nMore than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\nWard 250 runs under a Department of Energy authorization, not a Nuclear Regulatory Commission license, and the DOE program it belongs to covers research and demonstration rather than commercial sale.\nValar itself is a 2023-founded outfit from El Segundo, California, reportedly backed by Oculus founder Palmer Luckey and Palantir CTO Shyam Sankar, with CB Insights data putting its valuation around $2 billion as of March.\nLuis is committed to sharing his expertise and enthusiasm with enthusiasts and industry professionals alike, with a firm belief in the continuous evolution and innovation driving the auto industry forward.\n\n### gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06\nMicron’s stock is up about 855 percent in the last 12 months, while Nvidia traded down over 15 percent from its June 2, 2026, high.\nIn fairness, June 2026 was a harsh month for many technology stocks.\nInterestingly, the S&P 500 is up about 9.5 percent year to date, but over the same period, the forward price-to-earnings ratio has contracted nearly 10 percent.\nThat is because earnings of the companies in that index have been incredible, with roughly an 18 percent growth rate for the first half of 2026, and Q2 expected to be above 20 percent.\nThat trend—rising stock prices and simultaneously improving valuations—is very bullish.\nFor earnings to keep improving, we need 1) lower oil prices (which seems to be happening), 2) the AI-related infrastructure buildout to continue (I suspect it will through the next 12 months), and 3) interest rates to stay where they are, or go lower (but the futures market says they will go higher).\nFor its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion.\nOne year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion.\nGAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago.\nGoldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\nUnfortunately, the AI boom is not just pushing up prices for the winners.\nA shortage of memory might affect the prices of consumer electronics.\nI’d say Micron is not the new Nvidia, just a new opportunity (talk to your financial advisor before making any investment).\nAllen Harris is an owner of Berkshire Money Management in Great Barrington and Dalton, managing more than $1 billion of investments.\n\n### gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06\nWhy AI factory economics approaching $100 billion per gigawatt make tokens-per-megawatt efficiency the decisive variable\nThe bull case of a compounding efficiency-plus-financing flywheel against the bear case of product-market mismatch and an unresolved split of the upside.\nThe News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories.\nIntroduced as a platform at GTC Taipei and now the design standard for the AI factories that Sharon AI and Firmus are building under the July 1 financing model, DSX reframes the gigawatt from a power budget into a revenue function.\nA CFO does not headline a product blog unless the company intends the announcement to be read as a financial-reporting event rather than a catalog addition — the debut of a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream that will surface in the financial model, not merely a new offering in the lineup.\nThe connection to the financing model is where the strategy becomes coherent.\nFinancing places NVIDIA silicon inside capital-constrained AI factories, and DSX ensures the resulting factories run at the token-per-watt efficiency that makes both the operator’s business and NVIDIA’s royalty viable.\nFirmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration is meant to improve tokens per watt and reduce cost per token for its customers.\nThat is an operator adopting NVIDIA’s revenue-per-gigawatt engine and NVIDIA’s financing in the same deal.\nThe $100 Billion Gigawatt Makes Efficiency the Whole Game\nHuang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\nWhen a gigawatt costs as much as $100 billion to build, an idle or inefficient watt is not a technical footnote but destroyed capital.\nIn that environment, revenue per gigawatt is the metric that decides whether a $100 billion facility earns its cost of capital, and tokens per megawatt is the lever.\nThe bull case is a flywheel: DSX raises revenue per gigawatt, the financing model spreads NVIDIA silicon into every regional and sovereign operator, and the recurring royalty compounds.\nThe hyperscalers and frontier labs on NVIDIA’s own Vera CPU adopter list are precisely the organizations capable of building alternatives with custom CPUs, open networking, disaggregated inference, or their own operations software.\nWhether the DSX-aligned factories reach the tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization the model assumes, and whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\n\n### gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06\nDuring its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\nFor 2027, that figure rises to 41%.\nAs a result, I think the stock could easily reach $300 per share sometime in late 2027.\nFurthermore, most of this growth is already known because management likely has good information on customer orders over the next year.\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 6, 2026 at 8:53AM EST\n\n### gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06\nThis shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nMemory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\nFor any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\nFirst shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nContract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\nBudgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\nAs rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts.\nNvidia's $7.8 million Vera Rubin rack rewrites AI profit map as GPU share shrinks ↗ · BigGo Finance\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record.\nExplore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\nThe same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for.\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older.\nSchneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\n\n### gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07\nMicron Technology ( MU +4.26% ) has had a phenomenal 2026 so far, rising around 240%, though it was up around 300% until a few days ago before artificial intelligence (AI)-centric stocks started to sell off.\nAs a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\nAs mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half.\nThat could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\nWhile the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\nNext quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\nMicron operates on a non-standard fiscal year calendar, and its FY 2026 ends in August.\nSo, I'll utilize FY 2027 projections.\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 7, 2026 at 9:30AM EST\n\n### gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07\nThis year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\nMicrosoft will provide investors with fiscal year (FY) 2027 capital expenditures guidance, and a huge jump over last year's figure could confirm the bull case behind Nvidia's stock.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); exec(\\\"for c in x[\\\\\\\"v5\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]); t=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]; print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t) if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|contract|price|2028\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/ladder-cost/article-candidates.json\\\")); d=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); exec(\\\"for c in x[\\\\\\\"v5\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"candidates\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n###\\\\\\\",c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"]); t=d.execute(\\\\\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\\\\\",(c[\\\\\\\"input_id\\\\\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]; print(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(s for s in re.split(r\\\\\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\\\\\s+|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",t) if re.search(r\\\\\\\"2027|contract|price|2028\\\\\\\",s,re.I)))\\\")'\"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5\n\n\n### gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2\nAs the DRAM crisis intensifies in 2026, graphics card prices continue to go up.\nSo as of right now, both US and European retailers are restocking the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with prices starting at $329 and €333, respectively.\nThis is the same price at which the RTX 3060 12 GB initially launched back in 2021.\nWe have seen prices for components and products swell sharply over the past few months, and everyone is signaling an intensification of this trend in the coming years .\nWhen looking at the prices of existing models, the RTX 5060 8 GB is available at $349-$359 .\nDespite the relaunch, the RTX 3060 is a vastly inferior GPU, & it's only saving grace would've been a sub-$300 US price, which it unfortunately doesn't have.\n\n### gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.\nCEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection.\nSome estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\nDemand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds.\nThe ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings.\nIf a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\n\n### gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512\nIf a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\nNearly all of the so-called Magnificent 7 stocks, plus Broadcom, are below their respective 50-day moving averages (Tesla being the exception), a sign that price weakness is becoming a trend.\nInterestingly, the S&P 500 is up about 9.5 percent year to date, but over the same period, the forward price-to-earnings ratio has contracted nearly 10 percent.\nThat trend—rising stock prices and simultaneously improving valuations—is very bullish.\nFor earnings to keep improving, we need 1) lower oil prices (which seems to be happening), 2) the AI-related infrastructure buildout to continue (I suspect it will through the next 12 months), and 3) interest rates to stay where they are, or go lower (but the futures market says they will go higher).\nIf a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\nUnfortunately, the AI boom is not just pushing up prices for the winners.\nA shortage of memory might affect the prices of consumer electronics.\nApple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\n\n### gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b\n\n\n### gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\nAccording to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation.\nBut the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\nContract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\nThe AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\nBudgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\nAs rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts.\nMorgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\nNvidia Vera Rubin rack hit with 435% memory price surge ↗ · Wccftech\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\n\n### gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e\nMicron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\nAs a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\nAs mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half.\nThat could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\nSo, I'll utilize FY 2027 projections.\n\n### gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42\nThe bank keeps a Buy rating and $350 price objective, arguing Nvidia's current multiple may already imply an unjustified 30% to 35% hit to future earnings.\nThe most surprising part of the report is not the bullish price target, but the valuation gap.\nBofA's valuation table also shows Nvidia at 15.7 times CY2027 earnings and 12.4 times CY2028 earnings, while Apple, Microsoft, Google, Amazon and Meta average 22.3 times and 19.0 times.\nIt estimates total ecosystem investments at around $65 billion, less than 35% of CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of CY2027E free cash flow.\n\n### gnews:c2ccca1a61b99adc685b1aa2\nIt also skips the pricey, scarce high-bandwidth memory that Nvidia’s chips lean on.\n\n### gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"question\":\"v4\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568\",\"day\":\"2026-07-04\",\"passage\":\"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\",\"why\":\"It gives the four companies’ planned 2026 spending.\"}\n{\"question\":\"v5\",\"input_id\":null,\"day\":null,\"passage\":\"\",\"why\":\"None gives a 2027-versus-2026 comparison for server DRAM and HBM contract prices.\"}\n{\"question\":\"v7\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b\",\"day\":\"2026-07-02\",\"passage\":\"Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\"why\":\"It gives Rubin’s production status and a possible 2026 shipping date.\"}\n{\"question\":\"v8\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02\",\"day\":\"2026-07-02\",\"passage\":\"The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\",\"why\":\"It states China’s position on access to H200s at that time.\"}\n{\"question\":\"v9\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45\",\"day\":\"2026-07-01\",\"passage\":\"In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\",\"why\":\"It gives a dated, quantified customer capacity backstop, though not the total outstanding at year-end.\"}\n{\"question\":\"v13\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568\",\"day\":\"2026-07-04\",\"passage\":\"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\",\"why\":\"It gives Nvidia’s 2027 projection for the same group and spending category.\"}\n{\"question\":\"v16\",\"input_id\":null,\"day\":null,\"passage\":\"\",\"why\":\"None gives cloud providers’ 2026 spending on custom AI silicon.\"}\n{\"question\":\"v18\",\"input_id\":\"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587\",\"day\":\"2026-07-05\",\"passage\":\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis\",\"why\":\"It reports a delay from the planned 2027 Rubin Ultra rack schedule; Nvidia disputes the report.\"}"}}
